---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Refraction from '../components/Refraction.astro'

const docsBase = import.meta.env.DEV ? 'http://localhost:4322' : 'https://docs.prismalens.io'

const steps = [
  'An alert arrives from Prometheus Alertmanager, or you write the incident yourself.',
  'PrismaLens copies the service\'s repository at its last commit. Your own checkout is left alone.',
  'Your coding agent investigates inside the copy. PrismaLens refuses its edits, deletes and mutating commands.',
  'You watch the run on the incident page while it happens.',
  'The report ranks the causes, shows the evidence for each, and lists what was ruled out and what to check next.',
]
---

<BaseLayout title="Your coding agent, investigating your incidents">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pt-16 pb-20 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.1] tracking-tight sm:text-6xl">
        Point it at an incident and the code behind it.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        PrismaLens opens an incident from your alert, hands it to the coding agent you already use, and
        keeps that agent on a read-only copy of the code. You get the likely causes in order, each tied
        to the file, line or commit that supports it.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-end">
        <pre class="rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-7"><span class="select-none text-muted-foreground">$ </span>npm install -g prismalens
<span class="select-none text-muted-foreground">$ </span>pl up</pre>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-6 pb-1">
          <a
            href={`${docsBase}/quickstart/`}
            class="rounded-md bg-primary px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-primary-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-primary/90"
          >
            Read the quick start
          </a>
          <a href="https://github.com/prismalens/prismalens" class="text-sm font-medium underline-offset-4 hover:underline">
            Source on GitHub
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Needs Node.js 24 or later on macOS, Linux or WSL2.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 sm:mt-20">
      <Refraction />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-border">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_2fr]">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What happens in a run</h2>
      <ol class="space-y-5">
        {steps.map((step, i) => (
          <li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_1fr] text-base leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-mono text-muted-foreground">{i + 1}</span>
            <span>{step}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pb-20 sm:px-6">
    <a href="/screenshots/report-0.5.0.webp" class="block">
      <img
        src="/screenshots/report-0.5.0.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="1246"
        alt="The PrismaLens report for the run above: root cause, culprit commit, coverage of what was and was not checked, the supported hypothesis with its four pieces of evidence, and the next step"
        class="w-full rounded-lg border border-border"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </a>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The report for the run above, as the 0.5.0 dashboard shows it. The coverage panel names what the
      agent did not check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-border">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_2fr]">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why not just ask your agent?</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">For a one-off question, do. PrismaLens is for the rest of the incident.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-x-10 gap-y-8 leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2">
        <p><strong>Nobody pastes the alert.</strong> Alertmanager posts to PrismaLens, and a critical or high alert on a known service starts a run. You can change that per service.</p>
        <p><strong>It uses the agent you already have.</strong> OpenCode by default, or Claude Code, Codex or Gemini CLI. PrismaLens never calls a model and has no account to sign up for.</p>
        <p><strong>Your checkout stays yours.</strong> Every run gets its own copy of the last commit, with any agent config committed in the repository removed.</p>
        <p><strong>It reads code, not dashboards.</strong> 0.5.0 gives the agent the alert and the repository. It does not connect to your metrics, logs or traces.</p>
        <p><strong>It tells you where the limits are.</strong> The read-only policy is a guardrail, not a wall, and runs are unsandboxed until you add a sandbox. The report says which you got. <a href={`${docsBase}/trust/`} class="underline underline-offset-4">What it reads and never does</a></p>
        <p><strong>It keeps the record.</strong> A week later the report, the evidence and every command the agent ran are still on the incident.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-border">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-4 px-4 py-16 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between sm:px-6">
      <p class="text-lg">The quick start takes about ten minutes and needs no alert source.</p>
      <a
        href={`${docsBase}/quickstart/`}
        class="self-start rounded-md bg-primary px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-primary-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-primary/90 sm:self-auto"
      >
        Read the quick start
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
